      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1882, the town was officially named Gladstone in honor of British Prime Minister William Ewart Gladstone.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.gladstonend.org/about",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The confluence of the Green and Heart Rivers served as a military camp site during the 1864 Sully Expedition and 1876 Custer’s Seventh Cavalry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.gladstonend.org/about",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "When the Northern Pacific Railway arrived, Gladstone blossomed into a bustling prairie hub, fueling regional growth.",
